From: Keenetic SDK Builder <developers@keenetic.com>
Subject: [PATCH] compat: keep one blake2s header on zinc kernels

compat.h now includes <crypto/blake2s.h> in every translation unit (the
kernel 6.19 blake2s rename block). On kernels < 5.10 the module uses
zinc's blake2s, and a kernel that ships its own crypto/blake2s.h — the
Keenetic NDM 4.9 one does — then defines enum blake2s_lengths and struct
blake2s_state a second time, so zinc's own translation units stop
compiling. The include path cannot fix this: LINUXINCLUDE is searched
before the module's ccflags.

Take zinc's header there and claim the kernel header's include guard, so
later <crypto/blake2s.h> includes (messages.h, cookie.c) are no-ops.

Signed-off-by: Keenetic SDK Builder <developers@keenetic.com>
---
diff --git a/src/compat/compat.h b/src/compat/compat.h
index 965795a..1107150 100644
--- a/src/compat/compat.h
+++ b/src/compat/compat.h
@@ -1434,7 +1434,18 @@ static inline char *nla_strdup(const struct nlattr *nla, gfp_t flags)
 
 /* Kernel 6.19+ renamed blake2s_state to blake2s_ctx and changed blake2s() arg order */
 #if LINUX_VERSION_CODE < KERNEL_VERSION(6, 19, 0)
+#ifdef COMPAT_CRYPTO_IS_ZINC
+/* The module uses zinc's blake2s on these kernels. Take zinc's header — before
+ * the argument order remap below, so its static inline blake2s() is parsed
+ * first — and claim the kernel header's include guard: a kernel that ships its
+ * own crypto/blake2s.h (Keenetic NDM 4.9 does) would otherwise define enum
+ * blake2s_lengths and struct blake2s_state a second time.
+ */
+#include <zinc/blake2s.h>
+#define _CRYPTO_BLAKE2S_H
+#else
 #include <crypto/blake2s.h>
+#endif
 #define blake2s_ctx blake2s_state
 #define blake2s(key, keylen, in, inlen, out, outlen) \
 	blake2s(out, in, key, outlen, inlen, keylen)
